  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Breezy and colder weekend with rain and snow \nshowers downwind of Lake Erie \n\nAnother vorticity lobe currently over NW PA per satellite\nimagery will swing through the mean trough across CPA and drive\na reinforcing shot of cold air advection (CAA) to the southeast\nor downwind of Lake Erie. The CAA along with a more favorable\nW/NW low level wind trajectory will promote lake effect precip\nthis weekend across the Northwest Allegheny Plateau and Laurel \nHighlands. Snow accumulations will generally be less than an \ninch and limited to the higher terrain, though isolated amounts \nof up to 2 inches will be possible by Sunday morning in areas \nthat see the most persistent snow bands. Brisk winds also  \nthrough the weekend will add a winter-like chill factor with \nmax gusts in the 20-30 mph range. \n\n------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Another extended stretch of dry and seasonal \nweather next week\n\nLake effect rain and snow showers come to an end by Monday \nmorning as an upper level ridge builds into the region. High \npressure at the surface and aloft will provide dry conditions \nwith mainly clear skies for next week along with moderating \ntemperatures. Highs should reach the 55-65F range by the second\nhalf of next week or +10-15 degrees above the historical average\nfor mid November.",
